{"version_id": 54, "article_id": 6, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Science cooperation in times of conflict needs fairer rules", "title_zh": "冲突时期搞科研合作，规则得改得更公平", "topic": "science", "source": "nature", "source_url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03176-y", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/49705390741_8c22b1fd8a_b.jpg", "credit": "USDAgov · pdm 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 289, "reading_minutes": 1.4, "lead": "Scientific collaboration has often been seen as a field that rises above politics. But new tensions are making a researcher's institution look like a political label.", "lead_zh": "科研合作一直被视为能超越政治的领域。但新的紧张局势正让研究者的所属机构变成一种政治标签。", "preview": "Scientific collaboration has long been considered one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. Researchers from different countries", "preview_zh": "长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。不同国家的科研人员经常携手合作，哪怕他们所在国家的政府意见不合。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益影响国际研究，一个意想不到的后果正在出现：所属机构正在变成政治身份的替身。换句话说，一位科学家所在的大学或研究所，如今可能被当作其", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 44, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/audio/6/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 124.55, "size_bytes": 751104, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 324, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/data/versions/54.json", "paragraphs": ["Scientific collaboration has long been considered one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. Researchers from different countries have often worked together, even when their governments disagreed. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended result is appearing: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. In other words, the university or institute a scientist belongs to may now be judged as a sign of political loyalty.", "This problem was raised in a correspondence published in Nature on 6 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh, who works at Shanghai University in China, and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi, who is based at Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They argue that maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules. Their concern is that current practices may unfairly punish individual researchers for the policies of their countries.", "The writers do not declare any competing interests, so their argument is presented as an independent opinion. Their letter is part of a wider discussion in the scientific community about how politics affects research. Related articles in Nature have examined academic boycotts, changing research partnerships, and the way geopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink collaboration. Other pieces have suggested five ways to stop global politics from slowing science down.", "The topic matters because science depends on open exchange. If affiliation is treated as a political identity, some researchers may lose access to journals, funding, or partners. That could slow progress on problems that affect everyone, such as health and climate. The authors call for rules that are fairer, so that collaboration can continue even when countries are in conflict. Their message is simple: politics should not decide who is allowed to take part in science."], "paragraphs_zh": ["长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。不同国家的科研人员经常携手合作，哪怕他们所在国家的政府意见不合。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益影响国际研究，一个意想不到的后果正在出现：所属机构正在变成政治身份的替身。换句话说，一位科学家所在的大学或研究所，如今可能被当作其政治立场的标志。", "这个问题在 2026 年 10 月 6 日发表于《自然》杂志的一封通讯中被提出。作者是就职于中国上海大学的 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh，以及任职于中国金华浙江师范大学的 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi。他们认为，在冲突时期维持全球科学合作，需要更公平的规则。他们担心的是，目前的做法可能因为某些国家的政策，而不公平地惩罚科研人员个人。", "两位作者声明没有利益冲突，因此他们的观点被视为独立意见。这封来信属于科学界关于政治如何影响研究的更大讨论的一部分。《自然》杂志的相关文章探讨过学术抵制、研究伙伴关系的变化，以及地缘政治紧张如何促使中国重新思考合作。还有其他文章提出了五种方法，防止全球政治拖慢科学发展的脚步。", "这个话题之所以重要，是因为科学依赖开放的交流。如果所属机构被当成政治身份，一些科研人员可能会失去使用期刊、获得经费或寻找合作伙伴的机会。这可能会拖慢对所有人都有影响的问题的进展，比如健康和气候。作者呼吁制定更公平的规则，让合作在国家冲突时也能继续。他们的信息很简单：政治不应该决定谁有资格参与科学。"], "body": "Scientific collaboration has long been considered one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. Researchers from different countries have often worked together, even when their governments disagreed. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended result is appearing: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. In other words, the university or institute a scientist belongs to may now be judged as a sign of political loyalty.\n\nThis problem was raised in a correspondence published in Nature on 6 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh, who works at Shanghai University in China, and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi, who is based at Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They argue that maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules. Their concern is that current practices may unfairly punish individual researchers for the policies of their countries.\n\nThe writers do not declare any competing interests, so their argument is presented as an independent opinion. Their letter is part of a wider discussion in the scientific community about how politics affects research. Related articles in Nature have examined academic boycotts, changing research partnerships, and the way geopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink collaboration. Other pieces have suggested five ways to stop global politics from slowing science down.\n\nThe topic matters because science depends on open exchange. If affiliation is treated as a political identity, some researchers may lose access to journals, funding, or partners. That could slow progress on problems that affect everyone, such as health and climate. The authors call for rules that are fairer, so that collaboration can continue even when countries are in conflict. Their message is simple: politics should not decide who is allowed to take part in science.", "body_zh": "长期以来，科研合作一直被视为少数能够超越地缘政治的领域之一。不同国家的科研人员经常携手合作，哪怕他们所在国家的政府意见不合。然而，随着全球紧张局势日益影响国际研究，一个意想不到的后果正在出现：所属机构正在变成政治身份的替身。换句话说，一位科学家所在的大学或研究所，如今可能被当作其政治立场的标志。\n\n这个问题在 2026 年 10 月 6 日发表于《自然》杂志的一封通讯中被提出。作者是就职于中国上海大学的 Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh，以及任职于中国金华浙江师范大学的 Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi。他们认为，在冲突时期维持全球科学合作，需要更公平的规则。他们担心的是，目前的做法可能因为某些国家的政策，而不公平地惩罚科研人员个人。\n\n两位作者声明没有利益冲突，因此他们的观点被视为独立意见。这封来信属于科学界关于政治如何影响研究的更大讨论的一部分。《自然》杂志的相关文章探讨过学术抵制、研究伙伴关系的变化，以及地缘政治紧张如何促使中国重新思考合作。还有其他文章提出了五种方法，防止全球政治拖慢科学发展的脚步。\n\n这个话题之所以重要，是因为科学依赖开放的交流。如果所属机构被当成政治身份，一些科研人员可能会失去使用期刊、获得经费或寻找合作伙伴的机会。这可能会拖慢对所有人都有影响的问题的进展，比如健康和气候。作者呼吁制定更公平的规则，让合作在国家冲突时也能继续。他们的信息很简单：政治不应该决定谁有资格参与科学。", "text": "Scientific collaboration has long been considered one of the few areas that can rise above geopolitics. Researchers from different countries have often worked together, even when their governments disagreed. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended result is appearing: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity. In other words, the university or institute a scientist belongs to may now be judged as a sign of political loyalty. This problem was raised in a correspondence published in Nature on 6 October 2026. The authors are Emmanuel Chidiebere Edeh, who works at Shanghai University in China, and Ehizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi, who is based at Zhejiang Normal University in Jinhua, China. They argue that maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules. Their concern is that current practices may unfairly punish individual researchers for the policies of their countries. The writers do not declare any competing interests, so their argument is presented as an independent opinion. Their letter is part of a wider discussion in the scientific community about how politics affects research. Related articles in Nature have examined academic boycotts, changing research partnerships, and the way geopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink collaboration. Other pieces have suggested five ways to stop global politics from slowing science down. The topic matters because science depends on open exchange. If affiliation is treated as a political identity, some researchers may lose access to journals, funding, or partners. That could slow progress on problems that affect everyone, such as health and climate. The authors call for rules that are fairer, so that collaboration can continue even when countries are in conflict. Their message is simple: politics should not decide who is allowed to take part in science.", "vocab": [{"word": "collaboration", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合作", "note": "常与 international/global 搭配，指共同工作。"}, {"word": "geopolitics", "pos": "noun", "zh": "地缘政治", "note": "指国家间因地理位置和权力关系产生的政治。"}, {"word": "institutional affiliation", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "所属机构", "note": "学术语境中常指研究者所在的大学或研究所。"}, {"word": "proxy", "pos": "noun", "zh": "替身；代表", "note": "a proxy for 表示“某物的替代标志”。"}, {"word": "unintended", "pos": "adj", "zh": "意想不到的；非故意的", "note": "常与 consequence/result 搭配。"}, {"word": "correspondence", "pos": "noun", "zh": "通讯；来信", "note": "在期刊中指读者来信类短文。"}, {"word": "tension", "pos": "noun", "zh": "紧张局势", "note": "常用复数 tensions 指国家间的紧张关系。"}, {"word": "competing interests", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "利益冲突", "note": "学术论文中的标准声明用语。"}, {"word": "boycott", "pos": "noun", "zh": "抵制", "note": "academic boycott 指学术界有组织的拒绝合作。"}, {"word": "partnership", "pos": "noun", "zh": "伙伴关系", "note": "research partnership 指研究合作关系。"}, {"word": "funding", "pos": "noun", "zh": "经费；资金", "note": "指用于研究的钱，常用不可数形式。"}, {"word": "open exchange", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "开放交流", "note": "指信息、想法自由流动。"}], "title_original": "Maintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "summary_original": "Nature, Published online: 06 October 2026;\ndoi:10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules", "body_original": "CORRESPONDENCE\n06 October 2026\nMaintaining global science collaboration during conflict requires fairer rules\nBy\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\n0\n&\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\n1\nEmmanuel Chidiebere Edeh\nShanghai University, Shanghai, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEhizuelen Michael Mitchell Omoruyi\nZhejiang Normal University, Jinhua, China.\nView author publications\nSearch author on:\nPubMed\nGoogle Scholar\nEmail\nBluesky\nFacebook\nLinkedIn\nReddit\nWhatsapp\nX\nSave article\nView saved research\nScientific collaboration has long been regarded as one of the few domains capable of transcending geopolitics. However, as global tensions increasingly shape international research, an unintended consequence is emerging: institutional affiliation is becoming a proxy for political identity.\nAccess options\nAccess through your institution\nAccess Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals\nGet Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription\n$32.99\n/ 30 days\ncancel any time\nLearn more\nSubscribe to this journal\nReceive 52 print issues and online access\n$199.00 per year\nonly $3.83 per issue\nLearn more\nRent or buy this article\nPrices vary by article type\nfrom\n$1.95\nto\n$39.95\nLearn more\nPrices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout\nNature\n658\n, 572 (2026)\ndoi: https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-03176-y\nCompeting Interests\nThe authors declare no competing interests.\nRelated Articles\nAcademic boycotts: data hint at shifting research partnerships\nGeopolitical tensions are leading China to rethink research collaboration\nGlobal geopolitics should not stall science — 5 ways to push back\nSubjects\nScientific community\nPolicy\nCareers\nLatest on:\nScientific community\nPolicy\nCareers\nHow to mobilize the agile science needed in a crisis\nEditorial\n06 OCT 26\nRising numbers of Asian and African PhD students choose to stud